'''The Great Disappointment''' ({{Langx|ca|Gran Desengany}}) is the term applied by Illypnian historians to the discovery in 1564 that the western coast of the [[New World]] — the continents of [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]] — was already subject to colonisation by [[Fosia|Fosian]] powers approaching from the east across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The discovery was made by the [[Expedició del Ponent]] (Expedition of the West), a [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] exploratory fleet commanded by [[Berenguer de Montcada]], which crossed the isthmus connecting Lurandia and Abyala and encountered [[Kingdom of Zong|Zongese]] trading outposts and a major colony on the western mainland. Crucially, these Fosian settlements were found to be heavily garrisoned by fierce Sanese mercenaries known as the [[Sea Wolves]].

The revelation that the New World was not an exclusively Illypnian sphere of expansion, but was being simultaneously colonised from the opposite direction by civilisations of comparable or greater organisational capacity, produced a strategic and psychological crisis among the Illypnian colonial powers. This shock was deeply amplified by brutal early skirmishes with the Sea Wolves, whom the Volisanians terrifiedly dubbed the Shendan Demons. The event accelerated the pace of Illypnian colonisation of [[Abyala]], intensified colonial wars in the New World, opened new avenues of trans-oceanic trade, and introduced lasting patterns of suspicion and competition between the Illypnian and Fosian colonial spheres.

== Background ==

=== Illypnian colonisation of the New World ===
The Illypnian presence in the New World began with the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] discovery of [[Lurandia]] in the 1480s–1490s. Volisanian navigators, seeking westward trade routes to [[Fosia]], made landfall on the eastern coast of Lurandia and subsequently established the colony of [[Balisca]]. Other Illypnian powers — [[Zakan]], [[Echia]], [[Merica]], [[Breisland]], and [[Aetolia]] — followed in subsequent decades. The [[Kingdom of Burawa]], approaching from [[Kaftia]] rather than [[Illypnia]], established its colony of [[Lacashe]] on the eastern coast of [[Abyala]] in 1516.

By the 1560s, the Illypnian powers had colonised the eastern seaboards of both Lurandia and Abyala, the islands of the central ocean, and the isthmus connecting the two continents. The western coast of the New World, however, remained largely unexplored by Illypnian navigators. The existence of a second ocean — the [[Shendan Ocean]] — beyond the isthmus had been established by earlier expeditions, but no Illypnian fleet had conducted a sustained survey of the western coastline.

=== The Jade Sea War and its consequences ===
{{main|Jade Sea War}}
The origins of Fosian oceanic exploration — and, indirectly, of Illypnian exploration as well — lay in the [[Jade Sea War]] (c. 1300–1390), a ninety-year conflict between the [[Chengzhi Dynasty]] of the [[Kingdom of Daras]], the largest territorial state on [[Fosia]], and the [[Kingdom of Zong]], the dominant maritime power of the eastern seaboard. The Chengzhi dynasty sought to extend Darasian sovereignty over the eastern seaboard by invading [[Bingzhou]], one of the [[Three Luoyan States]] that was in personal union with Zong; Zong successfully repelled the Darasian advance, preserving its territorial integrity and that of its dependencies.

The military victory, however, came at a strategic cost. Daras, unable to conquer Zong, leveraged its control of the continental interior and its alliances with the western Fosian polities to impose a de facto trade blockade. Zong and the eastern seaboard were denied access to the overland and maritime routes that connected eastern Fosia to [[Illypnia]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world. The disruption cascaded westward: the volume of eastern luxury goods — silks, ceramics, tea, spices — reaching Illypnian and Mesogean markets contracted, and their prices rose. By the early 15th century, the trade networks that had connected the two ends of [[Adonia]] for centuries were severely diminished.

The blockade thus set two parallel processes in motion. In eastern Fosia, Zong — supported by the [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] federation and the [[Three Luoyan States]] — shut out from western markets, redirected its maritime energies eastward across the [[Shendan Ocean]] in search of new sources of wealth. In [[Illypnia]] and [[Southwestern Kaftia]], the contraction of the Fosian luxury trade drove navigators and their royal patrons to seek alternative maritime routes — first southward around [[Kaftia]] (the [[Imperial History of Burawa|Burawi circumnavigation attempts]] of the 1370s–1460s), then westward across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] (the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] discovery of [[Lurandia]] in the 1480s–1490s). Both expansions were responses, direct and indirect, to the same upstream disruption.

=== Fosian exploration and colonisation ===
The [[Kingdom of Zong]], the dominant power of the eastern maritime states, was the first to act. In the 1430s, Zong dispatched exploration fleets eastward across the [[Shendan Ocean]], drawing on the shipbuilding capacity, navigational knowledge, and experienced officer corps developed during the [[Jade Sea War]]. The Shendan Ocean, though vast, was navigable; the distance from the eastern Fosian coast to the western coast of the [[New World]] was comparable to the distance from [[Illypnia]] to the eastern coast of [[Lurandia]]. The [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] and the [[Three Luoyan States]], which had supported Zong during the war, followed within decades, establishing their own colonial presence.

Zongese navigators reached the western coast of the New World by the 1440s–1450s. Initial contact took the form of trading outposts — seasonal or permanent installations at harbour sites and river mouths, engaged in the exchange of goods with indigenous peoples. The outposts served as points of access to the resources of the interior: tropical hardwoods, precious metals, animal pelts, and agricultural products that were in demand in Fosian markets. The indigenous peoples of the western coast, who had developed their own maritime and riverine trade networks, participated in this exchange on terms that varied from cooperative to coerced.

The success of these outposts, combined with intensifying competition among the Fosian maritime states, prompted a transition from trading presence to territorial colonisation. By the mid-16th century, the [[Kingdom of Zong]] had established [[Tamsui]], a major colony on the western coast near the isthmus, alongside a network of smaller trading posts extending along the coastline. The [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] and the [[Three Luoyan States]], which had supported Zong during the Jade Sea War, established their own outposts further south along the western coast of [[Lurandia]]. The [[Kingdom of Daras]], though a powerful state in metropolitan Fosia, had been partially excluded from the initial colonisation by the prior claims of its rivals and the geographic disadvantage of its position further from the Shendan crossing.

The Fosian colonial presence differed from the Illypnian model in several respects. The Fosian states had longer experience of maritime trade networks across their home waters and were accustomed to operating trading diasporas — merchant communities established in foreign ports, operating under local licence or treaty arrangements. The transition from trading diaspora to territorial colony occurred gradually in the Fosian case, rather than through the rapid conquest-and-settlement model that characterised Illypnian colonisation of the eastern New World.

== The expedition ==

=== The Expedició del Ponent ===
In 1564, the [[Expedició del Ponent]] departed from the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] settlements on the isthmus connecting [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]]. The fleet, commanded by [[Berenguer de Montcada]], a Volisanian naval captain, was tasked with surveying the western coastline of the New World. The expedition was prompted by reports from earlier overland crossings of the isthmus that a large ocean — the [[Shendan Ocean]] — lay to the west, and by the strategic interest of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] in assessing the potential for western expansion.

The fleet crossed the isthmus by a combination of river navigation and overland portage, then launched into the [[Shendan Ocean]] from the western coast. Montcada sailed northward along the mainland, charting the coastline and recording the geography.

=== First contact with Fosian colonists ===
Within days of reaching the western coast, the expedition encountered Zongese trading outposts — small installations staffed by merchants and sailors from the [[Kingdom of Zong]], engaged in trade with the indigenous populations of the coast. The outposts were recognisable as colonial installations: they flew Zongese flags, maintained warehouses and harbour facilities, and were heavily garrisoned by Sanese mercenaries belonging to the Sea Wolves confederations.

Further along the coast, Montcada's fleet reached [[Tamsui]], a large Zongese colony of several thousand inhabitants. Tamsui was a permanent settlement with streets, markets, administrative buildings, temples, and a fortified harbour. The colony was governed by a Zongese official appointed from the metropolitan capital, and its population included merchants, soldiers, farmers, artisans, and their families. The scale and permanence of the settlement made it clear that the Fosian presence on the western coast was not a recent development but the product of decades of sustained colonisation.

The encounter between the Volisanian fleet and the Zongese colonial administration was conducted with cautious diplomacy. Neither side had anticipated the presence of the other; both had operated under the assumption that the New World was an extension of their own civilisational sphere, with only indigenous peoples as inhabitants. The Volisanians were confronted with a colony that displayed all the institutional hallmarks of an organised state — administrative buildings, a governor's residence, religious temples, a harbour fortified with cannon, and a population that included women, children, and civilian artisans, indicating permanent settlement rather than a temporary trading expedition.

Montcada exchanged gifts with the Zongese governor and the two parties communicated through a combination of gesture, interpreters drawn from the indigenous population, and the limited shared vocabulary that decades of indirect trade across the New World had begun to produce. Over a period of several weeks, Montcada's expedition surveyed the extent of the Zongese presence, mapping the coastline and recording observations on the colony's size, agricultural production, military capacity, and administrative structure. The Volisanians noted that the Zongese colonists maintained regular shipping contact with their metropolitan homeland across the [[Shendan Ocean]], and that the colony appeared to be growing rather than contracting.

Montcada was careful to avoid provocation. His fleet was small, far from reinforcement, and operating in waters unknown to Illypnian navigation. He made no territorial claims, offered no threats, and departed on amicable terms. The fleet returned across the isthmus to the Volisanian settlements, arriving in late 1564 with a report that would alter the strategic calculations of every colonial power on [[Adonia]].

== Reception and consequences ==

=== The Gran Desengany ===
Montcada's report reached the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] and the broader Illypnian world in 1565. The response was a combination of alarm and deflation. The New World — which the Illypnian powers had regarded as an effectively unlimited frontier for expansion, resource extraction, and colonial settlement — was revealed to be a contested space. The western half of both [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]] was subject to colonisation by Fosian states of comparable military and organisational capacity. The assumption that Illypnian civilisation had discovered a virgin continent was overturned.

The term Gran Desengany — Great Disappointment or Great Disillusionment — entered Volisanian usage to describe the psychological and strategic impact of the discovery. The term was subsequently adopted, in translation, by other Illypnian languages. In the [[Kingdom of Burawa]], the event was known as the Qalbi-jabka Weyn ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: the Great Heartbreak).

=== Acceleration of Abyalan colonisation ===
The immediate strategic response of the Illypnian powers was to accelerate the colonisation of the territories they already controlled. The concern that Fosian expansion might extend eastward across the isthmus or around the southern extremity of [[Lurandia]] — reaching the eastern coasts that the Illypnian powers had claimed — produced a sense of urgency that had been absent in the earlier decades of colonial expansion.

For the [[Kingdom of Burawa]], the Great Disappointment reinforced the strategic importance of [[Burawi Cusub]] and the [[Abyala Charter]]. The crown increased investment in the Abyalan colonies, expanded the garrison at [[Lacashe]], and intensified the campaigns against the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] and the [[Aruanãyára Empire]], both of which were conquered within the following decade. The consolidation of the Abyalan interior was motivated in part by the desire to establish a territorial depth that would be defensible against potential Fosian encroachment from the west. The [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] forts, originally established as trading and slaving stations, acquired a new strategic significance as outposts along the transatlantic route that connected the peninsula to Abyala — a route that, unlike the Mesogean, was not threatened by Fosian competition.

The Burawi response also had a domestic dimension. The Great Disappointment was invoked by the crown to justify increased taxation for colonial defence, and by the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] to reinforce the urgency of Christian missionary activity in the colonies. The Church framed the Fosian presence as a civilisational and spiritual challenge: non-Christian peoples were colonising the same lands that Burawi missionaries sought to convert, and the failure to act would cede souls as well as territory.

=== Colonial wars ===
The discovery of Fosian colonies in the New World introduced a new dimension to colonial warfare. Prior to 1564, colonial conflicts in the New World had been fought exclusively between Illypnian powers — disputes over boundaries, trade routes, and territorial claims within a shared colonial framework. After the Great Disappointment, wars between Illypnian and Fosian colonial interests became a recurring feature of New World politics.

The first conflicts were localised: skirmishes between Illypnian and Fosian trading outposts in contested coastal zones, particularly along the isthmus and the western coast of [[Lurandia]]. As both sides expanded, the conflicts escalated into larger campaigns involving metropolitan fleets and colonial armies. The colonial wars of the 17th and 18th centuries were shaped by the geographic reality that neither the Illypnian nor the Fosian powers could project decisive military force across the full breadth of the New World; the result was a protracted series of conflicts that produced shifting frontiers but no permanent resolution.

=== New trade ===
The Great Disappointment also opened new commercial possibilities. The Fosian colonies on the western coast produced goods — silks, ceramics, spices, lacquerware, and tea — that were in high demand in Illypnian markets but had previously been accessible only through the overland trade routes across [[Fosia]] or the maritime route via the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] and the [[Triphylian Isthmus]]. The presence of Fosian merchants in the New World created the possibility of a shorter trade circuit: Illypnian goods could be transported west across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] to [[Abyala]] or [[Lurandia]], carried overland or by coastal shipping to the isthmus, and exchanged for Fosian goods brought east across the [[Shendan Ocean]].

This trans-isthmus trade developed slowly during the late 16th century and expanded in the 17th, producing a new class of merchants — both Illypnian and Fosian — who specialised in the cross-continental exchange. The trade also facilitated the transmission of knowledge, technology, and cultural forms between the two civilisational spheres, though sustained diplomatic contact between the Illypnian and Fosian metropolitan powers was not established until later centuries.

=== Fear and suspicion ===
The Great Disappointment introduced a lasting pattern of suspicion between the Illypnian and Fosian colonial spheres. For the Illypnian powers, the discovery that the New World was already partially colonised by civilisations they had barely encountered produced a xenophobic reaction that persisted for generations. Fosian peoples were portrayed in Illypnian literature, art, and political discourse as competitors, threats, and rivals for global dominance. The racial and cultural categories that Illypnian societies constructed to describe the Fosian world were shaped by this initial encounter and the anxieties it produced.

The Fosian response was more measured, in part because the Fosian colonial powers had been aware of the existence of the Illypnian sphere for longer — the overland trade routes across [[Fosia]] had carried indirect knowledge of the Illypnian world for centuries, even if direct contact had been limited. The Fosian states regarded the Illypnian presence in the eastern New World as a strategic concern rather than an existential shock, and their colonial policies were adjusted accordingly.

== The voyages of Ernesto Lacunya ==
{{main|Ernesto Lacunya}}
In the decades following the Great Disappointment, Illypnian strategies shifted from sheer alarm to diplomatic and commercial adaptation, largely spearheaded by Volisanian navigators. The most consequential of these figures was Joan Ernesto Lacunya de Sants, who sought to secure safe Volisanian maritime routes across the contested Shendan Ocean. 

[[File:Ernesto_Lacuña.png|thumb|right|250px|Ernesto Lacunya, a pivotal figure in the founding of the city of Cinc Parets.]]

Beginning in 1605, Lacunya deliberately bypassed the heavily militarized Fosian colonies on Lurandia, instead navigating further west to establish [[Cinc Parets]] on the island of [[Dagit]]. Designed as a neutral, fortified trading warehouse rather than an aggressive colonial occupation, Cinc Parets successfully integrated merchants from Volisania, Zong, Fenia, and the Sanese archipelago into a bustling cosmopolitan hub. Lacunya continued to probe the northern boundaries of the Fosian sphere, successfully securing the Treaty of Naha with the island kingdom of [[Yanbaru]] between 1610 and 1613. 

However, his most significant contribution to post-Disappointment geopolitics occurred in 1615, when his galleon, the Matricia, wrecked on the coast of Mitoyagi. Encountering the fearsome second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], Lacunya offered his own life to spare his crew. This profound display of honorable conduct diffused the hostility and led to a landmark treaty that formally recognized the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a sovereign equal to the Volisanian crown. Through his highly influential 1628 memoir, Lacunya completely transformed the Illypnian perception of the Far East, replacing the terrifying myth of the Shendan Demons with a nuanced understanding of honorable, sovereign empires, and establishing a lucrative, state-controlled trade corridor between Cinc Parets and the Sanese capital.

== Historiography ==
The term Great Disappointment has been criticised by modern historians as Illypnian-centric. The event described — the encounter between two colonial spheres expanding in opposite directions — was experienced differently by the Fosian powers, for whom the discovery of Illypnian colonies in the eastern New World was a matter of strategic adjustment rather than psychological crisis. The indigenous peoples of the New World, who had experienced colonisation from both directions, are largely absent from the traditional historiography of the Great Disappointment.

Recent scholarship has reframed the event within the broader history of global contact, emphasising the role of indigenous intermediaries in facilitating the initial Volisanian-Zongese encounter, the pre-existing trade networks that connected the eastern and western coasts of the New World, and the agency of indigenous polities in navigating the competing colonial pressures.

